lowing clinical evaluation and duplex arterial study, CTA of the aorta and arteries of the lower extremity should be obtained. Popliteal artery aneurysms can affect blood flow to the legs and increase the risk of clotting. Discover symptoms and treatment solutions."}, {"title": "Popliteal and other aneurysms (excluding aortic aneurysms)", "url": "https://northeast.devonformularyguidance.nhs.uk/referral-guidance/eastern-locality/vascular/popliteal-and-other-aneurysms-excluding-aortic-aneurysms", "snippet": "Thrombosis causing sudden acute limb-threatening ischaemia (50% risk of limb loss): sudden acute ischaemia should be referred as a surgical emergency. Early intervention can save a limb. Sudden onset or worsening of intermittent claudication. Check contralateral popliteal artery – popliteal aneurysms are often bilateral. Embolus -Is patient in AF?"}]}
